        "y": 0.09,
        "width": 0.88,
        "height": 0.285
      },
      "highlights": [
        {
          "find": {
            "text": "deceptively projected high profitability based on undisclosed and baseless premises"
          },
          "fromFraction": 0.3,
          "toFraction": 0.65,
          "x": 0.1155,
          "y": 0.3316,
          "width": 0.6976,
          "height": 0.0237
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s6b",
      "imagePath": "performance-factor-announcement.png",
      "narrationText": "Pacific Life's own press release bragged about it. The first two Performance Factors it ever calculated were two-point-four-six and four-point-one-eight. At the company's advertised ten percent cap rate, that meant illustrations could show effective returns as high as twenty-four point six percent — or even forty-one point eight percent. The only number Pacific Life actually guaranteed? One-point-zero. No multiplier at all.",
