    {
      "id": "s6c",
      "imagePath": "cap-rate-cuts.png",
      "narrationText": "And the whole projection depended on Pacific Life earning a fifty percent profit on its own options trades — every single year, for thirty to fifty years. Four of its competitors — MetLife, New York Life, Northwestern Mutual, and OneAmerica — told regulators in writing that assumption was unreasonable. Within three years, Pacific Life cut its cap rate twice. Illustrated performance across the board dropped more than thirty-five percent.",
